  "worked_solution": "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=6t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{\u27e8g(t),{f}_{2}(t)\u27e9}{\u27e8{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\u27e9}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8g(t),{f}_{2}(t)\u27e9 & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $10{t}^{3}$ is an odd function, while ${t}^{2}+1$ is even. Hence, their product $10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-1,1],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{\u27e8{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)\u27e9}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =6t+0 \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
